title: Reports of the Junta's atrocities.
subtitle: The tortures in the Ziverbey Villa
view-if: year == 1972 and month = 3 and week = 1
max-visits: 1
tags: event
face-image: img/ziverbey.jpg
new-page: true

Life went on for the many after the 12 March 1971 memorandum and the junta-backed government of Nihat Erim was established. However, for the few that dared to speak up and act against the conventional, life had been crueler and more painful than ever before. The Socialist intellectuals who had previously backed the 9 March coup attempt and even those who had simply made their political tendencies known had been gathered in the Ziverbey Villa.

Ziverbey was a huge villa painted in white that stood menacingly in the heart of İstanbul. Originally built during the late Ottoman era as the residency of Zihni Pasha, now it is acting as a torturehouse under the eyes of the whole nation. But looking does not mean seeing, for the pains endured by these people go unnoticed while "life goes on" for the people couldn't look at the consequence of their silence in the eye.

"I couldn't see anything since my eyes were tied. Some people had laid me onto the floor and taken my socks off. A device was placed onto my wrists. I felt a screw being jammed. Such a moment came that I wasn't able to move my legs. Oil, or some other kind of liquid, they applied to the base of my feet. Then the birch rod started hitting. At first, I thought I could bear the pain (...) but the pain of bastinade is like nothing else (...) one feels a pain that sinks up to the bones. At the beginning of it, I was holding myself to not scream. But then I let myself free, because no matter how much you try, you cant keep your voice in. First the growling begins, then it turns into a cry for help, and you turn into an animal. It has such a spiritual aspect that adds onto the physical pain. You see yourself as disgraced..."

- İlhan Selçuk, writer and intellectual

"Our interrogation technique was "special". Our men were special... An incorrigible communist alleged that she was raped with a truncheon. Pardon my saying so, but would our 20-21 year old stout boys need a truncheon? It defies all logic. As far as I know, the prisoners were merely slapped around."

- Turgut Sunalp, commander of the 15th corps
  
- @root: Pain conquers what words can not.
